[#:seon.task{:name "a-pending-put-or-take-is-a-waiter-not-a-thread",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (flowlab/e0_put_threads.clj): from 4 threads, 10 pending put! -> 14 threads, 100 -> 104; 100 pending take! -> 105; an unbounded 100,000 put! loop reached ~7,500 threads and a machine load of 982. Cause: host/chez/java/async.ss:900-919, `jolt-async-put!` and `jolt-async-take!` fork a daemon thread that blocks in jolt-async-give/take whenever the op cannot complete at once. Everything built on them inherits it: mult (async.clj:891-900, put! per tap per value), pub/sub, mix, pipe, onto-chan, and core.async.flow's fan-out mults (flow/impl.clj routes-for). Correct: a pending put!/take! registers a callback waiter in the existing alt-handler protocol (alt-putters/alt-takers, async.ss:33-54, alt-deliver! 300-311), whose wake runs the callback on a fiber (fibers-async.ss's waiter, a third wake strategy beside the condvar and the fiber resume). No cap: a pending op costs a handler record. Replaces the per-op daemon thread.",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.async-test/a-mult-with-a-full-tap-costs-no-thread
               jolt.async-test/a-pending-take-costs-no-thread
               jolt.async-test/a-pending-put-costs-no-thread}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-channel-shows-its-fill",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (e6_quiescence E6.1): (datafy (chan 1)) is #<channel>, and a flow ping's ::flow/ins are opaque, so neither ping nor the dashboard can tell an empty in-chan from a full one, or see a parked put. Cause: async-chan (async.ss:73-76) holds items/cap/kind/closed?/alt-takers/alt-putters/takew but has no Datafiable. Correct: (datafy ch) answers {:buffer :fixed|:sliding|:dropping|:promise|:unbuffered :capacity n :count n :parked-puts n :parked-takes n :closed? b}, read under the channel mutex. Flow's ping, which datafies its ins and outs, then shows queue depth per port with no change to flow.",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.async-test/datafy-shows-count-and-parked-ops}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (claude_scratch/archive/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). The field's way (Go's runtime: 'all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!'): a process in which every thread and fiber is parked on a channel, promise or mutex, with no timer pending, no thread in a foreign call and no I/O wait registered with the poller, can never wake itself. Today it parks forever: the test runner's per-test tick budget (seon.proofs/test-vars, :seon.config.agent/test-ticks) counts work, and a parked test does none, so nothing ends it (the full run's shard 3: 61 min; agent_test's await-quiet: 1h45m). Correct, in Jolt (jolt/<topic>, root's go first: a dependency change): the scheduler detects global quiescence of the process and raises a typed error {:jolt/deadlock true :parked [what each thread waits on]} in each parked deref or <!! of the main carrier's waiters, which unwinds to its caller: in a shard, the test, which reports it as an :error with the parked stacks as its cause, and the shard goes on. A process waiting on a child's socket is in an I/O wait and is not quiescent; the child ends its own waits (seon.root/a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true) or exits (EOF), so the chain unwinds from the bottom. No clock. Proofs: a test that derefs an undelivered promise reports :error naming the promise and the next test runs; a test waiting on a live child's socket is not touched."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-main-carrier-is-stuck-only-when-no-thread-runs"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the deadlock design (claude_scratch/archive/jolt-deadlock-design-2026-10-25.md): jolt.host/main-carrier-run! raises stuck (host/chez/fibers.ss ~1676) when no fiber is queued, no timer set and no descriptor awaited, ignoring the OS threads a future or a Thread. still runs, so a fiber waiting on a working future is declared stuck. Seon's agents and one-thread test shards run in that mode. Correct, in Jolt (jolt/<topic>): count the OS threads the runtime forks (%ls-fork-thread, host/chez/lazy-bridge.ss ~342, where each is born and dies) and declare stuck only when none is running beside the main carrier. Proofs (Jolt): a fiber waiting on a working future is not stuck and gets its value; a fiber with nothing that can wake it is still stuck."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-adapter-runtimes-buffers-are-freed-at-the-real-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the audit of host/chez's dynamic-winds (Jolt jolt/a-read-scope-holds-across-a-park dfdc33ef: every winder whose cleanup closes or frees what its body uses takes jolt-finally-in, which a park or preemption drops, so the cleanup runs at the real exit; a plain winder runs at the park, and the resumed body meets a closed port or freed memory, as a fork server's zipdir-read did at boot). Two are left, in host/chez/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss: its buffer winder near line 555 (foreign-free buf; its before-thunk zeroes the buffer, so it also runs again on every rewind) and the one near line 666 (the Windows environment block, free block). That file loads before values.ss defines jolt-finally-in (fibers.ss captures the marker by guarded reference for the same reason), so the marker cannot be named there as the others are: the setup moves out of the before-thunk, and the cleanup either takes the marker by the same guarded, late-bound reference or moves into a file loaded after values.ss. Proof: a preemption stepped through each body (the scheduler's quantum, as zip-index-preempt-test does for zipdir-read) answers the body's value, and the buffer is freed once at the end."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
